1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
Computer Output Microfilm (COM): How Digital Files Become Archival Film. A guide for Singapore records and archive teams

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:19,419
A computer output microfilm (COM) system writes digital files such as TIFF, PDF and JPEG directly onto archival 16 mm or 35 mm silver-halide microfilm, bypassing paper entirely.

3
00:00:19,419 --> 00:00:27,500
The result is a human-readable preservation master with an LE-500 life expectancy that needs no software or file migration to read.

4
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:31,260
Also called an archive writer or COM recorder.

5
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:38,880
Exposes digital images and computer-generated output, such as reports, statements and registers, onto film one frame at a time.

6
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:45,780
It is the reverse of microfilm scanning: scanning turns film into digital files, COM turns digital files into film.

7
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:52,500
Used to give scanned and born-digital collections an analogue copy that outlasts storage media and file formats.

8
00:00:53,500 --> 00:01:02,080
Part: Archive writer. What it does: Converts digital files into images on 16 mm or 35 mm film. Example: MD AW3 digital archive writer. Part: Processor.

9
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:10,432
What it does: Develops, fixes and washes the exposed film; processing quality decides whether it is archival. Example: MD PRO3 deep-tank processor.

10
00:01:10,432 --> 00:01:18,500
Part: Film and chemistry. What it does: Silver-halide archive-writer film rated LE-500, with matched developer and fixer. Example: MGD33 film.

11
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:24,977
ISO 18901: stability specification for processed silver-gelatin film; the source of the LE-500 rating.

12
00:01:24,977 --> 00:01:33,460
ISO 18917: residual thiosulphate limits after processing, verified by the methylene blue test. ISO 18911: extended-term storage conditions for processed film.

13
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:38,883
ISO 6199: image quality for microfilmed documents, checked against test targets written on each roll.

14
00:01:38,883 --> 00:01:43,500
Meeting all four makes COM output a preservation master rather than a short-term copy.

15
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:55,007
1. Permanent records: registers, court and land records, financial records. 2. Air-gapped resilience: film is offline by nature and cannot be altered by ransomware. 3.

16
00:01:55,007 --> 00:02:03,500
No format migration: readable with a lens and a light source. 4. A hybrid strategy: digital for everyday access, film for preservation.

17
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:11,402
Does a COM system need a separate processor? Yes. Exposed film must be developed, fixed and washed to ISO 18917 to be archival.

18
00:02:11,402 --> 00:02:20,261
Is COM output usable as a business record in Singapore? The Evidence Act generally admits business records produced and kept under documented, reliable procedures.

19
00:02:20,261 --> 00:02:29,500
Keep quality-control procedures for the COM process. Can COM film be scanned back to digital? Yes, on a production microfilm scanner whenever a new access copy is needed.

20
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:33,437
